<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tashkent Soviet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> Soviet was the council of workers' and soldiers' deputies in Tashkent, dominated by Russian railway workers and soldiers, which seized power in Russian Turkestan in November 1917. It excluded the Muslim majority from government and in February 1918 sent troops that destroyed the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> Autonomy. It became the core of the Turkestan Soviet Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tashkent-soviet.jpg" alt="Tashkent Soviet" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guy Cosnahan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tashkent_History_1913.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tashkent Soviet was founded on 2 March 1917, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>, at an inaugural meeting of thirty-five workers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-caspian-railway">Central Asian Railway</a>. It was headed by a technician, I. I. Bel'kov. The next day a meeting in the local Duma set up an Executive Committee of Public Organisations to manage what it called the sociopolitical and economic life of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Only two Central Asians, the lawyers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fayzulla-khodzhayev">Fayzulla Khodzhayev</a> and Tashpolad Narbutabekov, sat on that committee. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> was a divided city: the predominantly Muslim Central Asians generally lived in the Old City, while Russians and other Europeans lived in the New City, where the railway had been built. The Soviet set up its own provisions commission to fight what it called trader-marauders, a term soon applied to Central Asians alone, and it did not welcome a Muslim branch of the Social Democratic Party formed by Central Asian workers.</p><p>Many Central Asians nonetheless had high hopes of the post-tsarist era. The poet Sirajiddin Makhdum Sidqi wrote popular verse in support of the revolution, and veterans of the Jadid resistance to the draft of 1916, among them Munawar Qari, organised the Tashkand Muslim Council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the revolution the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> had little influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and no organisation of their own, so they worked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a>. A Regional Soviet was formed by the first Regional Congress of workers' and soldiers' deputies between 7 and 15 April 1917, while the Executive Committee of the Tashkent Soviet, dominated by Mensheviks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionaries</a>, held sway in the city. The Turkestan Committee appointed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> refused to recognise the Soviets, and the Soviets in turn questioned the legitimacy of its every attempt to assert authority.</p><p>After the July Days in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, and on 21 July the Tashkent Social Democratic organisation adopted the call for all power to the Soviet. With food shortages critical in September and distrust of the Provisional Government growing, a crowd of thousands elected a presidium headed by a Bolshevik. When the Provisional Government fell, power passed to the Soviets.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tashkent Soviet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It lasted a year, 1917–1918, before the forces of the <b>Tashkent Soviet</b> brutally crushed it; around 14,000 people, including many leading Jadids, were killed in the ensuing massacre.</blockquote><a href="/movement/jadidism">Jadidism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After the October Revolution, the <b>Tashkent Soviet</b>, dominated by Russian settlers, excluded Muslims from power.</blockquote><a href="/state/kokand-autonomy">Kokand Autonomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Tashkent Soviet</b> took power in late 1917 and crushed the Kokand Autonomy, the Turkestan Soviet Republic was proclaimed in April 1918 within the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic, covering the former Turkestan Governorate-General.</blockquote><a href="/state/turkestan-assr">Turkestan ASSR</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Russian Revolution of 1917 the <b>Tashkent Soviet</b> seized power, and the region became the Turkestan ASSR in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/turkestan-governorate-general">Turkestan Governorate-General</a></li></ul></section>
